Consensus round on the dao.jing.dht epic. Positions weighed: author r1+r2 (gemini-3.1-pro-high), gpt-6-sol critique, and my own round-1 critique. Where I change my round-1 position, I say so explicitly.

**D1. Fragmentation home.** FINAL: **(c) — `IDhtNet` over a `dao.stream.udp` port** (unchanged from round 1). `fragment-envelope` is private (`udp.cljc:151-157`) and the machinery that makes it safe — chunk sizing (`udp.cljc:168-195`), oversize refusal (`udp.cljc:225-233`), bounded reassembly with eviction (`udp.cljc:316-361`), source-address-only replies (`udp.cljc:373-399`) — is reusable without duplication only if the DHT rides the channel; a node-local copy (author r2 slice 1 still specifies exactly that) is a second security-sensitive framing implementation in one tree, and gpt's (b) run as an *extraction* is the acceptable fallback, not (a) as written. (b) **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: acceptable only as one shared fragmenter extracted to a common namespace that *both* `dao.stream.udp` and the DHT require — not a copy — with DHT message identity aligned to the envelope's id semantics and an explicit request/answer marker replacing `direction-of`'s `:dao.stream.remote/op` test (`udp.cljc:143-148`); it costs three per-host socket stacks that (c) avoids. (a) **REJECT** as specified (copying a private envelope shape into `node.cljc` duplicates the one thing the round agrees must not be duplicated).

**D2. Wire codec.** FINAL: **CBOR now, conditionally on D1=(c)** — a refinement of my round-1 "decide/freeze before a fleet exists": the decision is downstream of the transport fork, and the fork resolves to (c). If the DHT rides the §3.2 channel, "One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile" (`dao.stream.remote.md:363-366`) makes CBOR not a choice but the channel's rule, and keeping Transit would mean carrying Transit text inside CBOR fragment envelopes. The clean break without version negotiation (`dao.jing.cbor.md:496-503`) is free only while no fleet exists, so it must ride slice 1. Author's switch-now **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION** (scope: wire only — `IDhtNet` at `dht.cljc:53-86` and the store handle unchanged; Base64-in-application-value stays per `dao.jing.cbor.md:464-471`; `dao.jing.cbor.md:469`'s DHT-envelope sentence updated with it). gpt's keep-the-wire **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: only under D1=(b)/(a), where the DHT owns its framing and `dao.stream.transit` is genuinely portable (`transit.cljc:6-10` has clj/cljs/cljd branches); under (c) it contradicts the channel rule.

**D3. Replication acknowledgement contract.** FINAL: **(a)** (unchanged). The author's r2 converged here in substance — "The replication queue is ephemeral and lost on process crash" — which resolves the round-1 contradiction; what remains is to make the sentence exact and pin the local backend. Reasons: `memory-log` is process-lifetime by its own contract (`memory_log.cljc:3-6`), replication is idempotent and convergent (`dao.jing.dht.md:83-86`) so intent is re-derivable from the local store, which is therefore the *only* durable record needed; a durable intent log (b) duplicates that record, adds an fsync-class cost per put to the path this epic exists to unblock (`dht.cljc:216-223`), and creates a second thing to reconcile after crashes. Where intent lives: a volatile `memory-log` of **content addresses** in the handle (addresses, not peer targets — the put path's synchronous `lookup`, `dht.cljc:214-215`, must move to the runner too; see D8); the durable basis is `:local`, and "durably stored locally" may only be claimed when `:local` is `dao.jing.file` — the generic constructor today accepts mem (`dht.cljc:285-311`), so the `dht:` spec must pin file and the docstring must condition the word. (b) **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: coherent only if the owner explicitly wants "acked ⇒ held on ≥1 remote peer," and then as an explicit file-backed intent stream recorded before ack, with defined retry/recovery/shutdown — I would not build it for this epic; **REJECT** as the default.

**D4. Offline / local-only.** FINAL: **(b) solo `IDhtNet`** (unchanged) — and I note this is now effectively 2-of-3: gpt's policy B2 independently defines local-only as "no listener, outbound dial, or replication worker," citing the same evidence I did — `create-node` binds a socket and starts a daemon receiver unconditionally (`node.cljc:308-332`, `start-receiver!` at `node.cljc:144-200`). The author's r2 (a) still ships the socket+thread and patches policy on top ("runner drops the replication request if there are no known peers"), which is nominal OFF, and drops-per-request is wasted work for a state that should not exist. Solo is a one-screen value (`known-peers []`, `lookup []`), makes "no socket opens" assertable in an acceptance test, and makes "never enqueue while offline" structural rather than a runner courtesy — the alternative queue-and-grow is unbounded because a memory-log never evicts (`memory_log.cljc:7-9`). (a) **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: only as a transitional shim, and only if the default path provably binds nothing. (c) **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: coherent only under D3=(b); under (a) there is nothing to retain — offline ⇒ never enqueue, and a later restart sweep or explicit re-publish covers peers appearing late. **REJECT** under D3=(a).

**D5. Non-JVM hosts.** FINAL: **(b)** (unchanged). This is D1 seen from the host axis: under (c), non-JVM support is two thin seams feeding `receive!` for exactly the primitives `dao.stream.remote.md:386-388` names (Node `dgram`, cljd `RawDatagramSocket`; browsers have no UDP), the browser path becomes a channel swap (ws §3.1 / pair relay §3.3), and — decisive with D6 — hardening is written **once** at the stream layer instead of three times. The author's (a) triple-versions the wire, codec, reassembly bounds, and rate limiting across hosts, which is where security defects concentrate. (a) **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: as a strict interim only — same wire/codec/fragmenter/hardening core shared, per-host code limited to the socket calls, superseded by (b) when the channel lands; **REJECT** as three independent peers as specified in the author's r2 slice 3.

**D6. Hardening timing.** FINAL: **design gates slice 1; implementation lands before any non-loopback exposure and before host spread** — I accept the author's r2 slice-4 *ordering* for the implementation while keeping my round-1 design-first gate, and gpt's A4 ("an architectural threat-model decision followed by implementation work") shows all three now agree on design-first; the residual is only whether it gets written down at slice 1. Evidence for the gate: fragmentation turns today's ~12× worst case (~1200 B reply to a ~100 B request, `node.cljc:76-80` and `node.cljc:190-195`) into ~60× (64 KiB across ~60 datagrams, `udp.cljc:65-68`), so the amplification budget, cookie scheme, and caps must be named in slice 1's contract even if coded in slice 4. The author's r2 cookie is now concrete and mostly right (stateless HMAC, LRU cache); adopt it with gpt's additions: bind the HMAC to observed source **ip:port** + a time bucket (not IP alone), and match replies to the expected source, not merely the rpc number (`node.cljc:176-178` delivers by `:rpc` only) — a per-`claimed-:from` bucket is spoofable, as gpt says. Author's late-slice **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION** (conditions: slice-1 written threat model; loopback-only until landed). My round-1 stance stands; no change of mind, only convergence in wording.

**D7. Root discovery.** FINAL: **out of this epic** (unchanged) — and the author converged (r2's slice list contains no root slice; their r1 already placed it "strictly OUTSIDE the DHT"). The epic's goal — `dht:` as *this process's* persistent store — is fully served by `<dir>/HEAD` (durable findings §2, line 16); root facts need writer authentication that does not exist (peer names are unauthenticated until ShiBi, `dao.stream.remote.md:503-506`), a naming/rendezvous layer that is proposal-only (`dao.stream.discovery.md`: "Nothing here is implemented"), and a signed monotonic chain is precisely a root of authority the DHT is forbidden to be (`dao.jing.dht.md:11-15`). gpt's signed-chain design (`{writer-id, sequence, previous-root, manifest-address}` over a configured stream, verified key + monotonicity) is the right seed — carry it verbatim into the separate epic's notes. gpt's in-epic slice **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: only if the owner explicitly widens this epic's goal to cross-process sharing *now*, and then without any rendezvous dependency (configured descriptors only); **REJECT** inside the REPL-store epic.

**D8. Missed defects — in or out?** FINAL: **all three in, split across slices.** (1) Synchronous `lookup` on the put path (`dht.cljc:214-215`, each hop 500 ms × 3 tries, `node.cljc:236-241`) — **in, slice 2**: the outbox carries content addresses and the runner owns peer resolution; without this the outbox only removes half the blocking. (2) Lookup stranding live peers behind dead closest-peers (`dht.cljc:107-131`; named open at `dao.jing.dht.md:180-184`) — **in, slice 4**: it is the read path this epic's foreign-fetch goal exercises, and under partition each missing address can cost k × 1.5 s; prune-and-expand to k *responsive* peers. (3) Storage limits / availability repair — **split**: in-epic is the *local* bound knob with explicit refuse/degrade when hit (a node that accepts content it cannot hold is lying; today caching is unbounded, `dao.jing.dht.md:172-174`), plus an optional bounded "republish local content on join" (which is also D3(a)'s restart sweep); **out** of this epic: the full repair/republish subsystem and GC/pinning — same deferral class as round 1, and the single-owner availability story is HEAD + the local file store. gpt's fuller availability scope **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION** (the bounded republish option), **REJECT** as an in-epic subsystem.

