(1) **Default `mem`, `dht:` explicit — all three agree; RESOLVED.** Ratify as written, with gpt's addition that a `dht:` choice names a durable directory (it does: `dht:<dir>`), and my addition to document the three postures (mem / `file:<dir>` / `dht:<dir>`).
(2) **Network off by default = solo net — 2-of-3 with the author's substance converging; RESOLVED** (it is D4; the residual is implementation shape, not policy). gpt's "bootstrap addresses are ordinary peers, never authorities" and my "announce the bound host:port when a socket opens" are compatible additions; adopt both.
(3) **Structurally enforced, not documented — FINAL: structural** (unchanged; gpt's B3 independently reaches the same mechanism: "A warning alone does not enforce it"). I adopt gpt's broader definition of sensitive — *anything the REPL can publish that the user has not explicitly marked public* (source text, literals/secrets, AST rows, execution state, metadata, index nodes) — over my narrower "everything the indexer publishes," because the broader phrasing extends correctly to future intake kinds even though the sets coincide today (`yin/repl/index.cljc:111-115` publishes code+provenance). Mechanism: `dht:` with bootstrap is fetch/cache/serve-only; originating publication of locally indexed content requires a second explicit opt-in (e.g. `--dht-publish`), with a clear statement that peers can retain and redistribute every segment — irreversibility is the reason this must be a gate, not advice: no deletes by design (`dao.jing.dht.md:13`), replication to k strangers, integrity never confidentiality (`dao.stream.remote.md` §1, §7). The author's r2 stance is documentation-only: **REJECT** as sufficient; it is fine as the *wording* that accompanies the gate.

